The international community continues to utter shock and indignation after the appearance of evidence that Russian forces have committed atrocities against the civilian population in Ukraine, and Moscow rejected these reports as "provocation".
“Responsibility of murdered, ravaged and seriously wounded by Russian troops of Ukrainian civilians is guarded by convicts,” New Zealand Prime Minister Jasinda Ardern told journalists in Wellington on April 4.
"Russia must answer to the world for what they have done," she added, noting that its government will discuss additional measures to support Ukraine in its fight against the Russian invasion.
Japanese Prime Minister Fumio Kishida called the incidents "a violation of international law."
Statements were made after reports that hundreds of civilians had been shot and thrown into fragrats or left on the streets in the suburb of Kyiv in Bucha by russian armies that had emerged from the area after the occupation for several weeks.
Photographs, which supposedly depict the bodies of executed civilians with tied-knacked people, shocked many and led to calls for increased sanctions against Russia and the criminal prosecution of the perpetrators.
French President Emmanuel Macron said in a radio interview on April 4 that there were signs that Russian troops committed "military crimes in Bucha.
"Which requires a new round of sanction and very clear measures," Macron said, adding that additional sanctions should be aimed at Russia's exports of coal and oil.
Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez said that Russian troops could go so far that they have committed "genocide" in Bucha.
"We're going to ensure that those who committed these war crimes don't go unpunished," Sanchez said in Madrid.
Served on state television late April 3, Russian Foreign Minister Maria Zakharova rejected the charges as "provocation".
Without evidence, she stated that the United U.S. and NATO "brought" an image to discredit Russia.
"In this case, I don't think the fact that these statements were made in the first minutes after the appearance of these materials does not raise any doubts about who "brought" this story," Zakharova said.
Earlier, the Ministry of Defense of the Russian Federation also without evidence stated that the image of Bucha is "another statement of the Kiev regime" and that all Russian troops left the city until March 30.
Moscow asked the U.N. Security Council to gather on April 4 to discuss what it called "provocate of Ukrainian radicals in Bucha.
Russia's Investigative Committee on April 4 unveiled a statement declaring an "investigation" on charges disseminating Ukraine's "knowingly false information" about the actions of russian troops in Bucha.
President of Ukraine Volodymyr Zelenskiy spoke on April 3, accusing Russian forces of committing "genocide" in the city, and told the Kremlin leaders that they should come to Bucha to see what their military did.
"I want all the leaders of the Russian Federation to see how their orders are being enforced," Zelenskiy said in a video address, switching from the Ukrainian language to Russian.
And there is a common responsibility.
For these assassinations, for these Tortures... For the shots in the back of the head," he said.
He stated that Russian President Vladimir Putin and the Russian military should be responsible for the actions of troops in Ukraine.
"When we find people connected behind our hands and beheadings, I don't understand," he said about the scenes of the casualties scattered on Bucha, a city about 35 kilometers northwest of Kyiv.
Correspondent of the Ukrainian service of Radio Svoboda on April 2 saw on the streets of a small city the bodies of allegedly civilians.
Only in one place the correspondent saw up to 10 bodies on the street.
UP journalists saw the bodies of at least 21 people in different places of Bucha.
The bodies of one group of nine people – all in civilian life – were scattered on the ground near a place that, according to local residents, was exploited by Russian forces as a base.
The casualties seem to have been murdered from a close distance.
In general, the Ukrainian authorities stated that the bodies of at least 410 civilians were found in the area of Kiev, which was controlled by Russian troops until last week.
Guess, it's all there, we've bought everything on the list.
"They were told: there is no Kyiv". How the occupiers vilely took Ukrainians to Russia and Belarus
The Russian invasion of Ukraine is accompanied by terrifying things: robberies, rapes, assassinations, torture.
In this list there is another point, about which now there is much less information - the removal of the local population to the territory of the enemy.
Approximately since mid-March, the russian occupiers "evacuated" Ukrainians from the temporarily occupied settlements to their territory and the territory of Belarus, which self-propelled President Lukashenko actually vested under a Russian military training ground.
"Ukrainska Pravda" has found the exported Ukrainians and their relatives to ask how the "evacuation" takes place and whether there are any ways to return to Ukraine.
The heroes of this text adhered to voluntary-comprehensive evacuation outside Ukraine under psychological pressure and from hopelessness.
Lucubles, they are alive and keeping in touch with their relatives.
However, due to the statements of the Ukrainian authorities, many citizens were exported to Russia and Belarus under fierce coercion.
Thus in Melitopol, the russians kidnapped the staff of the second maternity hospital, took children from there without their parents, including 12-year-old Myroslav, the daughter of the deceased champion of Ukraine in swimming Joseph Zachepynskyi.
Oleksandr's, Marina's family and their 10-year-old daughter Valya moved to Gostomel a few months before the war.
Alexander got a job at Antonov airport, which is located 2.5 kilometers from the village.
The family settled on the territory of the military town, although it was civilian.
As true Plast members, Denys and Marina collected anxiety suitcases in advance, but they did not have time to evacuate on the morning of February 24.
They didn't have their car, and suburban buses don't take anyone.
Approximately 12 o'clock in the afternoon of February 24, they saw helicopters with the Latin letter V, followed by the first projectiles.
One of them hit the next house.
Then the family descended into the basement and stayed there for a long three weeks, until March 17 until the russian military took them to Belarus.
In total, about 40 people hibernated.
Not everyone decided to go.
"At 6 p.m. on February 24, people came to the entrance, who spoke not in Russian and not in Ukrainian.
They ask: "I'm here?" – I say that yes. – "Walk!".
They searched me, they asked me in the basement, whether there were weapons.
All men were interrogated if they served in the army.
The women were told: "We came to protect you at the behest of Ramzan Kadyrov."
It was a Chechen riot riot, not even troops, young boys for 25-35 years.
They said: "Ukrainians, Sashko Bilyi (commanded a detachment of the UNA-UNSO "Viking", who fought on the side of Chechens in the first Russian-Chechen war - UP), now we have come to help you."
On the 3rd day, they asked what we were lacking.
We'll say water, there was a big problem with her.
They squandered the store, took the goods from there under the prepretext of "everythingfully Russian harness" and brought us 6 boulevings.
There's still a video on the Internet, where children thank Kadyrov for their food.
That's why they brought sausages after looting and say: "We understand that this is wrong, but we really need a video for Ramzan Akhmatovich."
Nobody particularly wanted to speak, but they made a beautiful slicing.
Our child there says: "You have 7 days to give up our phones" – they laughed.
They constantly separated themselves from the russians.
They said, how good it was that they came, because they didn't want war, they support Ukrainians and generally good: "Putin is a freak, Kadyrov too, but we can't do anything, because our families are there."
And mostly they didn't fight, but went robbing shops, robbing.
Chicken was brought to itself, one was tied to one leg by St. George's ribbon, and to the other – its white tape and called it "militia".
In Ukraine, they did not arrive at all.
I don't know how their intelligence worked... When they were beaten in Bucha, so that they didn't even get the dead, they asked us: "Do you have artillery?"
Moreover, we had consolidated detachments – Chechens among each other did not know each other, in the early days they questioned passwords to find their own or someone else's.
They we had until March 13, after which a Russian riot riot arrived, followed by Omsk troops.
They supplied 30-40 units of equipment between the houses - ours constantly fell into it from Bayraktars.
However, they came and told us that the Armed Forces of Ukraine no longer exist, but there is "Azov", and, accordingly, "Azov" so thousand 100 in Ukraine.
"Kiev has already surrendered" was promoted since the beginning of March.
Somehow a Russian came to us – either a paratrooper officer or a FSB representative – and says that there will be an evacuee.
And we listened to the radio – there was about Bucha, Gostomel, we thought, maybe we were given a "green corridor".
At the very very beginning, we handed over our lists to Chechens, they promised to contact the Ukrainian command.
But we are told: "You will be taken to Belarus, and then, perhaps, to Rostov."
We're saying we don't want there or there.
To which they responded: "Presolve the psyche of your children!"
As it worked: they drove us into the basement and started firing from the house - either "Grade" or with mortars.
And then flew back... Neighbourary houses just burned, some of them collapsed.
In our house there was a direct hit to the third floor.
Later, they agreed to smuggle us only to Belarus, voicing them to the border guards and the Red Cross.
Some didn't leave, saying that it was for traitors to their homeland - they say, "you will be shot there, they will sell to the organs."
We were transported through Chernobyl, on both sides of the road there were arrhythmiaptics, squandered equipment, although the Belarusians said that the russians were immediately exporting equipment to them.
There are a lot of capillaries, turgging equipment and soldiers.
We underwent improvised control at the Belarusian Komar checkpoint, many were without documents at all, because they burned down.
We were placed in the tents of the Red Cross, they gave tea.
And here we hear the gunshots! The missile is firing, and you can see a trace – Iskander worked in Kiev.
Although these from the Belarusian Red Cross said: "Yes, it's the planes to fly, turn around at the border."
But we from aviation, we have an education, we understand what it is.
Then we were loaded into the bus and taken to the sanatorium "Chonki" near Gomel.
Its leader, Venger Vasily Stepanovich came, he said: "Vot, I'm Khohol, I'm Chernigov.
But I will be hired that Putin and Lukaszenko should not be found, why your wolves will not please.
Bedny people Stradiet!
Lukashenko is so good!
Let's tell me, so it'll be."
It was proposed to give an interview to the Belarusian journalists, but no one wanted.
Sanatorium administration told us: "Yes you are already traitors!"
People from the Red Cross and United Nations offices (at least they were so-called) circulated disinformation that men could not go to Poland.
Many people believed in this and were afraid to leave Belarus.
However, we decided to leave, although we had a problem with the documents – my daughter did not have a foreign passport.
We should receive it on Monday, and on Thursday the war broke out.
In the Ukrainian conslebgo consleb he did not help us anything, and in Minnesota at the station they said that without passports no one would take anyone on the bus.
Here I will say that Belarusian volunteers helped us a lot.
We were accommodated in Minnesota, and escort was carried out.
In the sanctuary did not hold power.
To our second group the migration representative said that you can live here for a maximum of a week and a half, because Belarus is not Europe, there are no payments here.
We found an internal carrier who volunteered to smuggle us to Varshava.
Driving near Mozyr (approximately 50 kilometers from the Ukrainian border - UP), we have seen ballistic missile launches on Ukraine: the missile first flies, glows beautifully, and then extinguished.
Residents who boarded the bus in Mozyr said that the russians are constantly fizzing landfills there.
But I can say that Belarusians absolutely do not want to fight at all.
Generals are resigned from the army.
One woman told us that he would break something to his son if he was called up.
However, the northern part of Belarus, where Minsk, do not believe at all.
They say: "You are lying, we are a peaceful people."
Don't assume that their landfills are used to shell Ukraine.
