Last week, the Verkhovna Rada Adopted Draft Law 7176, which is entitled "Concerning the Monitoring of Potential Threats to Ukraine's National Security in the Sphere of the Sphere of the Economy".
Surprisingly, in fact, the only potential threat to Ukraine's national security in the sphere of the economy turned out to be... a place for whom the meetings are held by members of the supervisory board of state-owned companies and state-owned banks.
Initiators of the draft law believe that decision-making by the Supervisory Board of the state-owned company in conditional Kyiv can somehow solve the problems of Ukraine's national security.
We are aware of a large number of new dangers for state-owned companies, but it is not yet known about a company that had a need to physically hold a meeting of the supervisory board at the location.
Potential Threat from such a law is understandable – this is a great basis to "destroy" all achievements in the difficult reforms of state-owned enterprises after the Revolution of Dignity, including non-independent supervisory boards, and then to establish manual management.
That's what it's until 2015.
Why should state-propelled supervisory boards and who should join them?
Moreover, if under patriotic guiding "froze" one malicious bill, then tomorrow they can fly and others - for example, you can demolish the system of public procurement through ProZorro or the independence of the NBU.
A lot can be attributed to the war.
By the way, this initiative appeared even before the beginning of the war and the bill 7176.
Thus, on February 22, this norm was included in the comparative table of draft law 5397.
And in general, the desire to disperse supervisory boards in one way circulates among some people's deputies for a long time.
Not for the sake of criticism as such, but in order to show the possible consequences of the draft law, we will analyze the arguments that explained the need for its adoption.
And then we will submit our proposals on how to resolve the issue of management of state-owned enterprises during the war.
The First Deputy Minister of Economy Denys Kudin emphasized the necessitation of such an innovation with the risks of losing the Internet and other means of communication, which will make it impossible to remotely operate supervisory boards.
In other words, the risk is that the Supervisory Board will not be able to make the necessary decisions, in connection with what can be paralyzed the work of the company can be paralyzed.
Agree that there is such a risk, but will he be able to prevent the relocation of members of the supervisory boards to Ukraine?
Let's imagine a rather cynical situation, when independent members of the supervisory boards (depreciating the majority of the entire composition) will put their personal safety higher than the interests of the company and will refuse to come to Ukraine.
In this case, in accordance with the draft law, they can be released.
And then, most likely, this will lead to the loss of a quorum necessary for the supervisory board to make any decisions.
Instead, it will not be possible to quickly appoint new independent members of the supervisory board, since the law provides for the need for a competitive selection, which lasts an average of 3-4 months.
Therefore, the decision proposed by draft law 7176 will most likely not be able to protect the opportunity to make a decision from loss by the supervisory board, but rather, on the contrary, it will lead to this!
People's Deputies, the author of the bill Dmytro Natalukha submits other arguments in his support.
In particular, he notes: "many enterprises, in particular the defense-industrial-industrial complex, now require relocation", and therefore "it is difficult to imagine that a person can, for example, discuss addresses and other sensitive information from Vienna by Zoom."
To begin with, in Ukraine there are almost almost no state-owned defense companies that have supervisory boards.
The Supervisory Board exists only in Ukroboronprom, which, in fact, manages almost all state-owned enterprises of the defense industry.
But even in Ukroboronprom, in accordance with the legislation, the Supervisory Board has quite limited powers, and all its members perform their duties not free of charge.
All the fullness of power in Ukroboronrom belongs to the General Director, who, by the way, will decide on the need to relocate this or that defense industry enterprise, which is part of the concern.
In addition, such an argument does not absolved criticism also because bill 7176 does not make any changes to the legislation regulating the activities of state-owned enterprises.
On his Facebook page also cites arguments that can be attributed as "emotional".
Such arguments, as a rule, have nothing to do with increasing the efficiency of state-owned companies and state-owned banks, but let's try to disassemble some of them: "referential foreign experts at the first word "war", in 2021 they leave Ukraine, but at the same time they remain members of supervisory boards in state-owned enterprises of Ukraine with a full salary of several hundreds of thousands of hryvnias, while Ukraine itself is really suffocating."
Firstly, it is manipulation – the majority of such members have never resided in Ukraine and only periodically came here.
Therefore, to say that they "leave Ukraine", to put it mildly, unfairly.
Secondly, as a result of the coronavirus pandemic, both foreign and Ukrainian members of Supervisory Boards have long resorted to the practice of holding online meetings.
This tool has long been widespread in world practice, and in completely different areas: business, education, medicine, etc.
We should also not forget that membership in the Supervisory Board is not full-time.
As a rule, such people have a different employment, and just means of electronic communication help them in this.
Thirtly, all these statements are read as claims exclusively to foreigners.
I wonder why Mr. Dmitriy does not make identical claims to members of the supervisory boards who are Ukrainians?
After all, they also do not at the location of their companies.
"By the way, this is not the first populist attempts of deputies to remove foreigners from the supervisory boards, and the war is only a new concise opportunity.
