U.S. Prohibits Investment in Russia and Impose Sanctions Against Putin's Daughters
The United U.S. together with the G7 and the EU introduces new sanctions against the daughters of Russian President-Putin, Russia's largest banks Alpha Bank and Sberbank, as well as prohibit all new investments in Russia.
This is stated in a statement on the website of the White House.
Sbierbank is the largest financial institution of Russia, owned by the government, and Alfa-Bank is the largest private bank.
Sanctions will froze any of their assets related to the U.S.'s financial system and ban U.S. citizens from doing business with them.
In addition, the United States will impose a complete ban on new investments in the Russian Federation.
To this end, U.S. President Joe Bieden will sign a new decree, which includes a ban on new investments in Russia by U.S. citizens regardless of their location.
"This step is based on the decision adopted by more than 600 multinational companies to withdraw from Russia.
Private sector exit includes manufacturers, energy companies, large retailers, financial institutions, as well as other service providers such as legal and consulting firms," the White House said.
The third-throup of new sanctions from the United U.S. were restrictions against major Russian state-owned critical enterprises.
This will prohibit U.S. citizens from carrying out operations with these organizations and freeze any of their assets in U.S. jurisdiction.
A detailed list of the Ministry of Finance will be published the next day on Thursday.
And the last part of the package is the complete blocking of the assets of the adult children of Putin, the wife and daughter of Foreign Minister Lavrov, as well as members of the Russian Security Council, including ex-president and Russian Prime Minister Dmitry Medvedev and Prime Minister Mikheil Mishustin
