Friends are old and not very, friends, with whom I do not know personally, friends in spirit and intelligence.
Thankful times also come to you too.
Over the past month, I've talked to many of you.
Your life, which has already never been easy, turned upside down, like the life of every Ukrainian.
Many of you are fleeing from Russia.
And many of you have confessed to feeling guilty and embarrassed because of the actions of your country in relation to your neighbors.
Due to why they are subjected to Ukraine on your behalf.
Over some of you, Activists, the threat has long been loomed, and you were preparing for a decisive blow.
At the beginning of March I wrote to Alexander Cherkasov, my very old friend from the "Memorial".
I'll tell you a little later - as usual succinctly answered Sasha. - Now, after the search, we go through the ruins.
Others – cultural figures, artists, critics, writers – are impressed by the sudden collapse of your fragile world.
I don't like Putin and his regime of thieves and fascists, most of you hate them.
"But let's be frank: except for very few of you - those who worked at the "Memorial", "Novaya Gazeta", on "Echo Moskvy", in "Medusa", Navalny organization and in a number of other places - how many of you did at least something to resist this regime?
Except for participation in rallies, when they still took place.
READ ALISTRICAL ALSO a column of Russian journalist "Late Hid, Smoking Late Late"
And if so, you are sure that your sense of shame and guilt is not just an abstraction?
Perhaps they are caused by your prolonged indifference to what is happening around, your apathy and your passive complicity, who are now probably a great burden on your soul and the heart?
It didn't always.
In the 1990's, there was a short period when you to some extent possessed freedom and a democracy - uncompromising even bloody, but real.
But the year 1991 was not the buzz better in 1917.
Why whenever you finally have a revolution, you are eventually curbled by such a strong fear that you are looking for salvation behind a tsar's back, let his name Stalin or Pubin?
No matter how many people he kills, you still feel that it's safer. Why so?
Indeterminally, mistakes were made.
Instead of seizing and promulgating KDB's archives, as the Germans from the stratum did, you took your soul away at the Dzerzhinsky monument – and allowed the KGB to lie to the bottom, to renew itself, reorganize and seize power over the country.
"When you are faced with a choice between robbery of the country and the return of the Communists, you did not fight for the possibility of a third option - and humbly resigned to robbery.
In 1998, your economy collapsed and this marked the end of mass rallies for great social justice or against the war in Chechnya.
Rescructibility was survival.
Then Putin appeared. A young, enterprising, aggressive, he promised to deal with the terrorists and raise the economy.
Few of you bought up for it, but you still either voted for it, or preferred not to vote at all.
When he began to align Chechnya with the ground again, most of you turned a blind eye to it.
I remember those years well.
At the same time I worked in Chechnya, helping victims of Putin's "anti-terrorist operation", and with my own eyes saw the ruins of Grozny, Qatar-Yurta, Itum-Kala and other cities.
Sometimes at the weekend I returned to Moscow and cheerfully huddled cheerfully, my friends.
We drank, danced, and at the same time I tried to tell you about the territies I had witnessed: about the torture of the civilians, about the murdered children, about the soldiers who sold the bodies of the dead to their families.
You said to me: "Bride, we're already fed up with your Chechnya." I remember these words very well.
Response I resented: "Friends, it's not my Chechnya, it's your Chechnya.
That's your country, slumber, not mine.
I'm just a slurred foreigner.
It is your government that bombs one of your cities, kills your fellow citizens.”
But no, it's all too difficult, too painful and you didn't want to know anything.
Then there was an economic upswing of the middle of the zero, due to rising oil prices and Putin's willingness to look through his fingers that part of the stolen money will remain in the pockets of the middle class.
Many of you began to make good money, some got rich, and even the poorest of you have purchased a new home and found a better job.
The prices grew, but it's a misfortune?
Moscow shining festively and shrilled with chic.
When they killed a few op-positioners – Yuriy Shchekochykhin, Anna Politkovska, Oleksandr Lytvynenko and others – many of you were shocked and terrified about what was happening.
But it didn't go further.
When, after two terms, Putin passed the post of President Medvedev, and he took the place of the prime minister, you, as I see, barely paid attention to it.
When, after several months of Medvedev's rule, Russia invaded Georgia, most of you ignored it or were silent.
How many of you I met in the following years on Hudauri's mountain slopes, Kazbegi's foothills or in a café and in the Turkish bacillos of Tbilisi while part of this country was occupied by your army?
I have to admit that it is unlikely that in the West we have done a lot if we did anything at all.
Suffocated indulgence, a bit of sanction; but what is the point that Russia squeezfully violates international law when there is such a temptation of Russian oil, gas and the domestic market?
To live in Russia it was good.
And after the severe 1990s, it was the most important.
However, at the end of 2011 you, my Russian friends, still woke up.
When Putin swapped again with Medvedev and slept the presidency as before, many of you decided that it was already too, and you came up en masse.
Navalny's name became a household name, you didn't come from the streets for half a year, and the regime eventually felt with horror that he was losing ground under your feet.
After that, he stumbled in retaliation.
Initially, alternative actions were organized, then more resuscitatory laws were adopted, and the prisons were filled with people.
Thousand were behind bars.
Some got a huge deadline.
"And what could we do?"
I've heard it so often and I've heard so until now.
"The state is so strong, and we are so weak."
So, look at the Ukrainians.
Look at what they've done two years ago.
Exacercated by the pro-Russian president, who cheated on their European hopes, they once captured the Maidan – and have never left it again.
They quite independently erected a tent camp and prepared for decisive defense.
When the police came and tried to destroy it, they began to defend themselves with the help of throbes, reinforcements and Molotov cocktails.
Ultimately, the police opened fire.
But instead of fleeing, the protesters went into the attack.
Many died, but they were defeated.
Yanukovich became fugitive, and the Ukrainians regained democracy, the right to independently elect leaders and expulsify them if they do their job badly.
Putin didn't like the Maidan very much.
This was a bad example.
Therefore, taking advantage of the total turbulence, he captured the Crimea.
Some of you were opposed, but there is little benefit from this.
And how many of you were delighted!
As far as I don't know, 91% of Russian citizens supported the annex.
Suddenly a new myth was born, and a lot of you who despised Putin and his cache, suddenly returned 180 degrees and began to adore him.
It's hard for me to find a reason, because we stopped communicating immediately after that.
The rest of those who remained my friends were mostly silent.
"We don't care about politics," you said.
And you hid a new one in books, movies, IKEA catalogs and parks, completely like new after the restoration, which was started by the mayor of Moscow in 2012 – with their binbags, a publicly available Wuafia and hypster cafals.
In fact, the Donbas is far away, and Moscow is so good – and it is only getting better.
You barely paid attention to Syria.
There were terrorists, didn't it?
ISIS or anything... Even the Moscow editor, who released my book about Sirius, criticized it in an interview then, because I supposedly did not understand anything about what was happening in Syria.
Well, at least I've traveled there and I've seen on the streets of Homs state snipers cold-bloodedly shooting my children.
Among all the citizens of Russia, only your Soldiers, who in 2015 started bombing thousands of civilians, gaining experience for the next serious war.
Undoubtedly, many of you know the words pastor Martin Niemeller:
"At first they came for the socialists, but I was silent - because I am not a socialist.
Then they came to the members of the trade unions, but I was silent – because I am not a member of trade unions.
Then they came for the Jews, but I kept silent – because I am not a Jew.
Then they came for me, but there's no one else who could say anything in my defense.'
How many of you talked about Chechens, Syrians or Ukrainians?
Some of you did that.
But the vast majority remained silent.
Some are really talking now – like, for example, Dmytro Hlukhovsky, Mykhailo Shyshkin, Mykhailo Zyhar, Maksym Osypov and others.
The majority allows themselves to speak from abroad, some from the middle of the country, like Marina Ovsyannikova, chanting to get into a new Gulag or to make a company Navalny.
As for others, you best know in what country you live in.
Therefore, I am sure, you understand that when Putin disassemtutes with the Ukrainians – or that seems very probable, if he does not succeed – he will take up for you.
According to all of you, my friends: for those who courageously, but for the most part alone went to the protests and are still in a minor term, but will soon receive a more serious one.
For thousands of you who signed the petition, who expressed disagreement on social networks (albeit only a black square on Instagram) or spoke in private conversations with colleagues at work.
Times when 10 or even 25 years of imprisonment were given just for an anecdote, they did not remain in such a distant past – and now they are most likely await you in the future.
Who will speak for you then? Who will be left?
An example of Ukrainians - even more than in 2014 - frightens the Putin regime: they prove that it can be dealt with.
And that the mind, motivation and bravery can stop him, no matter how despicable his superiority is on paper.
Taking into account everything, few people in Russia are aware of this, as well as the fact that the war continues altogether.
But you, my friends, well know what's going on now.
You are reading foreign news on the Internet, you have friends or even relatives in Ukraine, with whom you keep in touch.
And Putin knows what you know.
Therefore, be ready.
You understand what everything is going for.
A gorgeous life has come to an end in exchange for your silence.
Your election is just a hochma, your laws, if you don't take into account resuscitators, worth nothing more than the paper on which they're written, your latest free media isn't there anymore, your economy collapses faster than I write, you don't don't have credit cards to buy a plane ticket abroad, even if some flights are still there.
Now Putin will not be satisfied with your silence, he will demand your consent, your obedience.
If you don't give him what he wants, you can try to either somehow leave, or you will be crushed.
Doubt that you see some other option.
And yet there's another.
Who will eventually collapse this regime.
And, perhaps, in the prevailing conditions, you will need fewer than you think.
Think about it.
Spark will not flaCreate from you: due to the economic collapse that looms against Russia, it will most likely light up in the provincial, in small cities.
When the prices will soar, and the salaries will not be paid, those people who voted for Putin all these years, because they wanted bread and peace, will come to the streets.
Putin knows this and is afraid of these people much more than he is afraid of intellectuals and mid-sedlear of Moscow and St. Petersburg, i.e. you, my friends.
But if each city goes to rallies in itself, as it happened periodically periodically, it will not be difficult for him to squeeze them alone.
Organizing and coordinating will be necessary. The crowd will need to be converted into a mass.
You will have this wonderful magical tool – the Internet, which mode can be limited, but which still works and which can be adjusted under almost any circumstances.
Navalny's organization was defeated, but it is possible to create other, more informal, more decentralized.
There are a lot of you, there are millions of you.
